#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5595c32-d167-5a2c-9469-972e442fece1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335467} {:answer/id #uuid "5b60de0c-c7aa-599a-8749-99ff0d614957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335468} {:answer/id #uuid "34bd2de0-93c2-5efd-a1fc-6f4f8b5a2dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335469} {:answer/id #uuid "63c160d0-8bc1-5f1d-8441-c1e9e370bed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335470} {:answer/id #uuid "54d14098-faa6-5881-afca-5a6442c450e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54d14098-faa6-5881-afca-5a6442c450e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335471} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7f66ee-621c-5010-bfcb-e5d681c9977f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef4e4fc3-8477-5354-a829-8e34e5c5f42e" :question/math-academy-id "q-14310" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ given that  $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C) \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9fdbbe-6c91-576e-964d-2dfe7094a9b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\tan(7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f9fdbbe-6c91-576e-964d-2dfe7094a9b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\tan(7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335474} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08ee092-354d-5b2e-a653-c757d24ab056" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1adf5c18-652c-5a35-ba00-6bf5024e9f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-355544" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${sec}^{2}⁡(4y){y}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x) \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=4{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e83f0dba-a96b-5eef-892c-e7bd97ee16f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^2-\\frac{5}{x}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335477} {:answer/id #uuid "63609b85-c6f5-5030-9499-a29f4e473a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^2-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83f0dba-a96b-5eef-892c-e7bd97ee16f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^2-\\frac{5}{x}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335477} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ee74b-194b-5ef8-a522-c1f51f015f52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26f23820-0414-5dbe-a547-4bdb9a3bb271" :question/math-academy-id "q-355546" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8{x}^{3}=5$ that satisfies $y(1)=6.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8{x}^{3} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5+8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}y & =(5{x}^{-2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\text{d}y & =\\int (5{x}^{-2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-5{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =6 \\\\ -5(1)^{-1}+4(1)^{2}+C & =6 \\\\ C-1 & =6 \\\\ C & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-5{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{2}+7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x}+4{x}^{2}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78623355-e38a-5ca6-82fc-0ede5c4c9a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{5}{4}-e^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78623355-e38a-5ca6-82fc-0ede5c4c9a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{5}{4}-e^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290366} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c75e092-6079-5be0-bdeb-81cf428bb1cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed0df24d-07b8-5d65-9f2d-72f21abe2584" :question/math-academy-id "q-355577" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ given that $\\,y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{0}+C) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ C-1 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e865fe4b-b552-5e49-96e0-af34995f5e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335481} {:answer/id #uuid "dd92b623-ea95-5559-b407-a6917af8dcc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335482} {:answer/id #uuid "860d2f15-f8dc-50f2-bc1d-877881de2c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{e}^{x}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335483} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b377a4-5170-5909-969a-fee5feb2b631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}+1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335484} {:answer/id #uuid "13db39f2-ff13-5346-83b5-dc1e2e7c1488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13db39f2-ff13-5346-83b5-dc1e2e7c1488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335485} :answer-field/id #uuid "e35eb022-54e0-5d0a-b4bd-e9bd48e358a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20669a20-777a-5d15-ade0-e739edb8a182" :question/math-academy-id "q-4016" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2y}} \\\\ {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{2y} & =2{e}^{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+C) & =0 \\\\ 2+C & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1).\n$$"}]}
